{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/architecture.md at exact head 520251692928e5f3e912ba454bad460c3a4567b2 on request/onboarding-identity-core, cut from main 92dd2df1. Candidate artifact for the documentation half of request 2c9bf640, under promise 2999b16a; the behaviour artifact for the same head is 3d3abd14 at host.\n\nWHY THIS PAGE CHANGED. The head adds a public package, host/identity, and the exported Go API an outside application imports is one of the six compatibility axes. Under the review rule on this page, a head that changes a layer's contract must update the page in the same head, so this is not documentation trailing the code — it is part of the contract change.\n\nWHAT IT NOW SAYS. A Host identity subsection under layer 4, Application host binding, because identity sits at that seam for the same reason the binding does: an application must not have to invent it, and a host must read it without already holding an application profile. It states the three fixed schema families; the anchor as a record saying one key belongs to a persistent identity for this repository within a scope until an expiry; the two axes and why they are reported separately rather than collapsed; that vouching is derived from the signature and never claimed in a payload; that a witness declaration is in force only from the initializing key, that the last authorized one wins, and that rotation does not reach back; that a witness cannot mint outside its declared schemes; that a delegation names no identity, inherits the endorser's, takes the weaker value on each axis, cannot outlive the anchor it rests on, and is withdrawn when that anchor is; that resolution is the authority and nothing gates appending; that expiry and withdrawal are judged against the log's own signed timestamps and never the reader's clock; and that the provider check runs outside the fold so replay makes no network request. It closes by saying plainly what this is not: it authenticates nobody and authorizes nothing, witness custody sits inside the supported single-operator boundary where every process in it can use that key, authenticated shared-host support stays deferred, and the two-axis display belongs to the application.\n\nAlso a host/identity row in the package-boundary table; the surface compatibility axis widened from the host package's exported surface to the exported surface of host and host/identity; and the layer-4/5 seam sentence extended to name host/identity among the packages that must stay free of any application profile.\n\nThe page's front matter gains the live host artifact 969a4e7a as a basis, which is the artifact for the behaviour this prose now describes. It resolves through the anchor gate, which requires a citation to be an artifact rather than a request, and it will flare when the successor at host lands — which is the mechanism working, not an oversight.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD. gofmt -l clean before commit; go build ./... ; go vet ./... ; go test -count=1 ./... green across 27 packages. The docset gate is included in that run and passes: front matter, no-empty-basis, flare, and the anchor gate that every named act resolves to a live record.\n\nPUBLICATION NOTE. I asked codex for the docs slot before filing this and had no answer while the frontier stood still; nothing has superseded fa35d881, so the fan-out lane has not landed. Because I authored this candidate, I can retire it myself at once if that lane needs the slot, and I have told codex so. It is not an obstacle anyone else has to route around.","body":{"branch":"request/onboarding-identity-core","commit":"520251692928e5f3e912ba454bad460c3a4567b2","path":"docs"}}